    "q-130240": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130240",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.9),$ compute $P(X>4).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.9),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.1)^{x-1}(0.9), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>4.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{0}(0.9)+(0.1)^{1}(0.9)+(0.1)^{2}(0.9)+(0.1)^{3}(0.9) \\\\  & =0.9999.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.9999 \\\\  & =0.0001.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
